# Every supply whose capacity buffer is sized and which says what it served, beside the most it
# could have served. One row per examined layer, `violates` where the draw is over the line.
#
# A supply cannot serve more than it can make
#   The rule: a supply cannot serve more than its rating plus the headroom it filed. The
#   nameplate is what it committed, the capacity buffer is how far it can run above that, and the
#   draw is what came out. `draw > nameplate + capacitySlack` is a supply that produced more than
#   it can produce, which is a filing error and never a fact.
#
# The supply's bound, never the demand's
#   "A draw does not exceed what was asked" is the rule this one is not, because a shared line's
#   output is partly somebody else's demand; see layers/drawn.sqlc, where the corpus's one
#   instance is quoted. Bounding the draw by the demand fires on a correct document, which is a
#   rule spending the reader's trust to say nothing.
#
# Three answers, borrowed from `pm:Fit`
#   Three answers and not two, borrowed from `pm:Fit` rather than invented here. Comparing
#   `draw_mode > n_mode + capacity_slack` compares two points, in a model whose thesis is that a
#   quantity is a range. `Fit`'s own annotation settles the shape and ends the argument:
#   "clearance: nameplate.low ≥ demand.high, the whole range clears; transition: the ranges
#   overlap; interference: nameplate.high ≤ demand.low, the whole range interferes. Total and
#   exhaustive ... `mostLikely` decides nothing here." A rule deciding on the mode decides on the
#   one number the schema has already ruled out for this shape of question.
#
# Only a whole-range excess is a violation
#   "Did it exceed" and "could it have" are separate answers, and only the first is a violation.
#   The whole draw range above the whole available range is a filing error with no defence.
#   Ranges that overlap are a document that cannot settle its own question, and reporting that as
#   a breach would accuse a filer of something their figures do not say, which is the fault this
#   file already names twice, above and below.
#
# The middle answer has no population on this corpus
#   That is worth knowing rather than hiding. Few examined rows carry a ranged draw or rating at
#   all, and those that do clear outright. `examples/witnesses/main.rs` is where the other two
#   states are exercised.
#
# In one unit, or not at all
#   The draw, the rating and the capacity slack are added and compared, so all three must be in
#   one unit; a row where any differs is left out, and arithmetic/draw_bounded.sqlc names it
#   `not comparable`. Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `draw_bound`.
#
# An unmeasured capacity buffer suspends the rule
#   It does not pass it. Some layers do not know how far they can stretch; coalescing that to
#   zero would turn "nobody looked" into "there is no headroom" and manufacture violations out of
#   a gap. A nameplate becomes a ceiling only once somebody says the buffer is empty.
-- pm:Jagged/pm:draw against pm:Nameplate/pm:amount plus pm:Nameplate/pm:capacitySlack.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT d.filing, d.layer,
           -- the whole draw above the whole of what the supply could make
           d.draw_low > d.n_high + d.capacity_high + 1e-9              AS violates,
           CASE WHEN d.draw_low  > d.n_high + d.capacity_high + 1e-9
                THEN format('served [%s, %s] against at most %s: the whole range is over the '
                            'line', d.draw_low, d.draw_high,
                            d.n_high + d.capacity_high)
                WHEN d.draw_high <= d.n_low + d.capacity_low + 1e-9
                THEN format('served [%s, %s] against at least %s: the whole range clears',
                            d.draw_low, d.draw_high, d.n_low + d.capacity_low)
                ELSE format('served [%s, %s] against [%s, %s]: the ranges overlap, so this '
                            'document does not settle whether the supply was overrun',
                            d.draw_low, d.draw_high,
                            d.n_low + d.capacity_low, d.n_high + d.capacity_high)
           END                                                        AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(layers/drawn.sqlc)
    ) d
    WHERE d.capacity_slack IS NOT NULL
      AND d.draw_unit = d.n_unit
      AND d.capacity_unit = d.n_unit
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'draw_exceeds_the_supply'
